@page "/docs/common/data"

@* Data and filters: the request a data component hands its provider and the result it gets back,
   loading from EF Core, fields, filters in code, the operators, text and case, and dates. Calling
   an API, the filter syntax, OData and the server's limits are on Data from an API (DataApi.razor).
   From the data foundations design (artifacts/izzyblazor-data-foundations-design.md) and Core/Data. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="data">
    <Lead>
        A <Code>Table</Code> or <Code>DataGrid</Code> with an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> asks it for rows with a
        <Code>DataRequest</Code>, and the provider answers with a <Code>DataResult</Code>. The request carries the range,
        the sort and the filter. It applies itself to an EF Core query, so the database does the work, and it never
        turns a value into code.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The request and the result</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The component calls its <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> each time it needs rows. The <Code>DataRequest</Code> it
            passes describes those rows:
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Skip</code>, <code>Take</code></td><td>The range: how many rows to skip, and how many to take (null for every row). <code>Page</code> gives the same range as a page number, from 1.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Sort</code></td><td>A list of <code>SortField</code>s, each a key and whether it's descending. A Table sorts by one column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Filter</code></td><td>The filter, or null. A Table's filter is a <code>Filter.All</code> of one condition per filtered column, plus any other conditions of a bound <code>Filter</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Search</code></td><td>Free text to look for in the searchable fields. A Table doesn't set it. A provider can set it from your own search box: <code>request with { Search = query }</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Cursor</code></td><td>When the component pages by cursor, the <code>Next</code> or <code>Previous</code> of an earlier result.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NeedsTotal</code></td><td>Whether the component needs the total count. It's false when the component pages by cursor, where the total is optional.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>CancellationToken</code></td><td>Canceled when a newer request replaces this one. Pass it on to your queries.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            The <Code>DataResult</Code> contains the rows. Add the <Code>Total</Code> for numbered pages, or the
            <Code>Next</Code> and <Code>Previous</Code> cursors when the component pages by cursor:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Result</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">From a database</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The simplest way needs no provider. Pass an EF Core query as a Table's <Code>Items</Code>, and the database sorts,
            filters and pages it. Write an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> when you need more, such as scoping the rows to the
            signed-in user per request, calling an API, or running your own search.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            In a provider, <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> does the work. It filters, searches and sorts the query, counts the
            matching rows when <Code>NeedsTotal</Code> is true, and lists the range. You pass EF Core's async methods to it,
            so IzzyBlazor doesn't depend on EF Core:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" HighlightLines="7-9">@FromDatabase</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The request carries the component's columns, which define how each key reads an item, so the provider never maps
            keys to properties. Each condition becomes a LINQ expression on the column's own property, with its value as a
            query parameter.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>For a list in memory, <Code>request.ToResult(list.AsQueryable())</Code> does the same.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>To filter, sort and take the range without counting, call <Code>request.Apply(query)</Code>.
                <Code>ApplyFiltering</Code> and <Code>ApplySorting</Code> do one part each.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                When the provider throws, the Table logs the exception and shows "Couldn't load the rows." with Retry; see
                <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/loading-data#when-loading-fails">When loading fails</Anchor>. A request the
                Table canceled isn't an error.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Fields</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A field is something the data can be sorted, filtered or searched by. It has a key, the expression that reads it,
            and the things it allows: sorting, filtering, search, and which operators. A Table's columns are its fields.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A <Code>PropertyColumn</Code>'s key is its property path in lower case (<Code>customer.name</Code>), unless it
            has a <Code>SortKey</Code>. A <Code>TemplateColumn</Code>'s key is its <Code>SortKey</Code>. It filters and
            searches by its <Code>FilterBy</Code> and sorts by its <Code>SortBy</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Where there are no columns, such as on a server, read the fields from a type with
            <Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code>. It returns each public top-level property a filter can compare, keyed by
            its name in lower case. Each field is sortable and filterable, text fields are searchable, and an enum's names
            are its choices.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The caption comes from <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The format comes from <Code>[DisplayFormat]</Code> and <Code>[DataType]</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Properties with <Code>[ScaffoldColumn(false)]</Code> or <Code>[Display(AutoGenerateField = false)]</Code> are left out.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>To choose the fields and what each allows, declare them yourself:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Declared</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To limit the operators a field allows, set its <Code>Operators</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A field's own <Code>Filter</Code> builds its condition in your code. Write one for a computed value, or a value
            stored differently from how it's shown. It gets the condition with its values parsed for the field's type, and
            returns the filter on the item, or null for the usual one. A Table column has the same <Code>Filter</Code>
            parameter:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@Custom</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>It runs for every operator, so check the operator you handle.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>It matches case, unless you lower both sides.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Filters in code</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A filter is a tree. A <Code>FieldFilter</Code> is a condition on one field. A <Code>FilterGroup</Code> joins
            conditions with and or or, and a <Code>NotFilter</Code> keeps what its filter doesn't. Each condition names its
            field by key, the same key the Table's URL uses: <Code>?f-status=</Code> filters the field <Code>status</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@InCode</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To set a Table's filter from C#, bind its <Code>Filter</Code> on
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">interactive pages</Anchor>. With an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>, the whole
            filter reaches the provider as <Code>request.Filter</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@Bound</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A condition that a column's filter can show appears in that filter:
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Column type</th><th>Conditions its filter shows</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Text</td><td>Contains</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Number</td><td>Between, at least, at most or equals</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Date</td><td>On day</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Choice</td><td>In</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            Any other condition, such as an or group, still applies. It appears as a tag the user can remove.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A provider that passes conditions on to an API reads them with <Code>Find</Code>. It returns the condition on that
            field that every match must meet, or null:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Find</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Operators</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            An operator sets how a condition compares the field's value. The operators a field allows depend on its type. An
            operator the type doesn't allow matches nothing.
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Types</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Equals</code></td><td>All</td><td>The value. With a null value, the same as <code>IsNull</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NotEquals</code></td><td>All</td><td>Anything but the value.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>In</code></td><td>All</td><td>Any of the values in a list.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NotIn</code></td><td>All</td><td>None of the values in a list.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>LessThan</code>, <code>LessThanOrEqual</code>, <code>GreaterThan</code>, <code>GreaterThanOrEqual</code></td><td>Numbers, dates, times</td><td>Values below or above the value.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Between</code></td><td>Numbers, dates, times</td><td>Values from the first to the second, both included. A null end has no limit.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>OnDay</code></td><td>Dates</td><td>Dates on the day in the value. See <Anchor Href="docs/common/data#dates">Dates</Anchor>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Contains</code>, <code>DoesNotContain</code></td><td>Text</td><td>Text that contains the value, or doesn't. <code>DoesNotContain</code> also matches null.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>StartsWith</code>, <code>EndsWith</code></td><td>Text</td><td>Text that starts or ends with the value.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>IsEmpty</code>, <code>IsNotEmpty</code></td><td>Text</td><td>Empty text or null; or text with at least one character.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>IsNull</code>, <code>IsNotNull</code></td><td>Any type that can be null</td><td>No value; or any value.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            "All" means text, numbers, dates, times, enums, booleans and Guids. Enums, booleans and Guids allow only the
            operators marked All, plus the null checks when they can be null.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Values are parsed for the field's type, so <Code>"10"</Code> filters a number and <Code>"Paid"</Code> filters an
            enum. A value that doesn't parse matches nothing, and so does a condition on a field that doesn't exist.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                Numbers are read first as the user writes them in the current culture (<Code>"1.234,5"</Code> in German), then
                in the invariant form (<Code>"1234.5"</Code>).
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                Dates and times are read first in ISO form (<Code>"2026-10-28"</Code>), then in the current culture's form.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                On a whole-number field, a fractional bound rounds the way the comparison means. "At least 2.5" matches 3 or
                more, and "at most 2.5" matches 2 or less.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>A bound beyond what the field's type can store matches every row or none.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Text and case</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Text filters ignore case by default, as a Table's filters do. To ignore case, IzzyBlazor lowers both sides of the
            comparison, and a database index can't serve that. Two settings change it: one for a single condition, and one
            for every condition on a field.
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Applies to</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>MatchCase</code> on a <code>FieldFilter</code></td><td>That condition</td><td>Matches upper and lower case exactly. For <code>Equals</code>, it matches spaces exactly too.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>FilterCase</code> on a Table or DataGrid</td><td>Every column filter and the search</td><td>Sets how text compares; see the values below. It reaches the fields through the columns.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Case</code> on a <code>DataField</code></td><td>Every condition on that field</td><td>The same values, for fields you declare.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th><code>FilterCase</code></th><th>How text compares</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>IgnoreCase</code> (the default)</td><td>Upper and lower case are the same. Both sides are lowered, which an index can't serve.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>MatchCase</code></td><td>Text is compared as it is. In memory, that's exact. In a database, the database's own comparison still applies: SQLite's <code>LIKE</code>, which EF Core uses for starts-with and ends-with, ignores ASCII case.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Database</code></td><td>Text is compared as it is, and the database's collation decides, so an index can serve it. On a list in memory, it's the same as <code>MatchCase</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            Text ignores case only when both settings allow it. A condition with <Code>MatchCase</Code> compares as it is,
            whatever the field's <Code>Case</Code>. A field whose <Code>Case</Code> isn't <Code>IgnoreCase</Code> compares as
            it is, even when the condition doesn't set <Code>MatchCase</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The filter's text form doesn't carry either setting. An endpoint that reads the filter, such as an export, sets
            <Code>Case</Code> on its own fields to match the screen. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/data-api#filters-as-text">Filters as text</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Dates</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>OnDay</Code> matches every date on one day. How it compares depends on the field's type:
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field type</th><th>How <code>OnDay</code> compares</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>DateOnly</code></td><td>The date equals the day.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>DateTime</code></td><td>The value is within the day's range, from its start to the next day's start, so an index can serve it.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>DateTimeOffset</code></td><td>The date on the clock where the value was recorded equals the day. EF Core can't translate this on SQLite.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            When a field has a time and the condition's value is a date alone, the condition means the whole day, not its
            first instant. <Code>Equals</Code> a date matches that day, and "on or before 3 Jan" keeps 3 Jan at noon.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
